00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.090
So before I answer that
question specifically, let's

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.470
talk about a little
bit of history.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.520
So I'm going to go through
three different stages of,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.670
like, how companies and
people use computers,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.970
and then it's going to end,
as you can see, in Cloud.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.670
And then we're going to get
into what that actually is.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.640
So first of all, we're going
to talk about the world

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.100
as we used to know it,
which is when companies

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.285
were in the on premise world.

00:03:04.285 --> 00:03:05.910
So basically, this
was back in the day.

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.920
Let's say I'm a startup,
and I'm spinning up

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.340
the newest, like,
social media website.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.880
Let's call it, like,
facenotebook.com or something.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.860
And I have five users.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.460
At that point, I have one
server sitting in my closet,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.560
and I have one person
just managing that server.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.900
Everything's fine because
I only have five users.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.260
The next day, though, word
spreads and I have 1,000 users,

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.090
and then my website
keeps crashing.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.130
And I'm like, why
is that happening?

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.110
And does anyone know the answer?

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:36.910
It's cause I only
have one server,

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.310
and one server is not going
to handle that traffic.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.200
So I have to do
something called scaling.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.180
And nowadays, we
think about scaling--

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.440
again, that's another buzzword
that startups love to use.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.530
But back in the day,
scaling was this--

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:55.030
it was I had to go to a store,
buy a brand new computer,

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.400
put it on top of the other
computer in my closet,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.190
download code on that,
do a bunch of other stuff

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.110
to add it to my
network, and then I

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.050
could now handle 1,000 users.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.820
And that took almost
an entire day.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.960
That was a huge headache.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.170
And so that was just--

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:14.470
that's basically one of the
things that lead to Cloud.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.829
So that pain point is what
led to the next thing, called

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:19.149
timesharing.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:21.614
So we have startups that
have these computers,

00:04:21.614 --> 00:04:23.030
but on the other
side of the aisle

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.700
we have these huge companies.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.480
Think, like, IBM, right?

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:27.820
And they have a lot of money.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:31.210
They can buy gigantic
sets of computers.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:33.220
They don't need to
guess and say, OK, I

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.480
have 1,000 users today.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.190
What if I have a
million tomorrow?

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:39.310
And then I'm going to buy that
many computers to basically

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.630
manage that scale.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:44.020
IBM, they can just go out and
buy a million computers, right?

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.150
But what if they
over-budgeted, and now they

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.210
have, like, 200 computers
that are not being used?

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.880
Then one smart person said,
hey, let's rent these out.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.410
Let's allow other companies
like these startups

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.440
to rent out these computers
for certain amounts of time

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.360
so that then it's
cheaper for them

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.770
and we're making passive income.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.190
And so that's how we got to
the idea called timesharing.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.020
And then this got
more and more popular,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.730
and then that just
became mainstream.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:14.120
It became very normal for
startups, smaller companies,

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.120
to go to large companies
and rent out their computers

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.200
for a certain amount of time.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.680
And then after a while,
these bigger companies

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.810
said, oh, what if we
could make more money off

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.370
of this timesharing thing?

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:27.650
What if we provided
additional services

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.040
on top of this infrastructure
that we're selling?

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.080
And these additional
services, this suite

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.540
of services that was created,
is what's called Cloud.

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:40.660
And so I didn't necessarily get
into the details of how people

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.180
use Cloud, but now I'm going
to get into that to hopefully

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.430
clarify what Cloud really is.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:48.400
Going one level deeper.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.530
So Cloud is oftentimes broken up
into three different umbrellas

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.850
of tools.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.560
And so the first set of tools is
what's known as Infrastructure

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:57.850
as a Service.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:59.860
Infrastructure as a
Service, that's timesharing.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.290
That is, I have a computer, and
I'm not using it all the time.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.470
So let me sell it for a
certain amount of time

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.450
to these other companies.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.010
I have storage disks.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.960
Let me sell this infrastructure.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.420
It's literally hardware
that you can touch and feel.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.151
So I'm selling that.

00:06:15.151 --> 00:06:16.650
And then, as I said,
these companies

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.080
said, what if we can
add additional tooling

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.150
to this infrastructure?

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:25.050
What if instead of saying,
OK, I have 10 users today,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.330
and then I have
100 users tomorrow,

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.120
and making the
companies manually scale

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.560
that on our network, what if we
just had a tool that said, hey,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:34.680
startups--

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.660
whether you have 10 users
or a million users--

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.209
we will handle that for you.

00:06:39.209 --> 00:06:41.250
You just need to write
the code for your website,

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.440
and we will fully scale for you.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.970
So that's basically
infrastructure

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.420
plus a little bit of magic.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.100
I throw those stars
up there to, like--

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.140
they're a little bit of
magic in the background.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.270
Because it's basically
infrastructure

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.390
with some software involved,
but it's quite magical.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.800
I like to think of it
that way, at least.

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:02.700
And then lastly is
Software as a Service.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.190
So these are basically
tools that you know.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:08.479
Think Google Docs, Google
Drive, Slides, and Sheets.

00:07:08.479 --> 00:07:10.770
There are also other companies
that are in the Software

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.810
as a Service space
like Salesforce,

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.100
for example, if
you've heard of them.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.720
And so this is basically-- these
are the three different tiers

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.630
or three different
umbrellas of Cloud.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:25.770
So now, what is Cloud?

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.764
I basically just
gave some background,

00:07:27.764 --> 00:07:29.430
then I talked about
the three umbrellas.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:30.960
So what is it?

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.320
In one sentence, Cloud
is a set of tools

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.500
that helps you spend
less time managing

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.140
and spend more time creating.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:42.430
So let me say it again.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:45.310
Cloud is just a set of tools
that helps you, the developer,

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.240
spend less time
managing and more time

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:52.130
being creative as a developer.

00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:54.130
So I don't need to
hire an entire staff

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:56.200
to scale my infrastructure.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:59.380
I can just pay Google
a little extra money,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:01.300
and they'll fully scale for me.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:02.980
So I don't need to
put time into that.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:05.140
I can put time into
building new features,

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.100
talking to my customers.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:09.860
That's what Cloud is.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:12.800
And so now, what is GCP?

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.310
GCP is obviously a cloud
tool, but it's many things.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.640
And so before I get into
the specifics of GCP,

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.460
let me talk about
Google Cloud as a whole.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:27.590
So Google Cloud is the mixture
of both GCP on the right

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.770
and G Suite on the left.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:34.429
And so GCP, in a basic sense, is
the infrastructure and platform

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:36.390
of the service
side of the house.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.510
So those are things
like, like I said,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.000
infrastructure and platform,
where you can run code,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.080
store data, things like that.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.140
And then G Suite, as
you all probably know,

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.570
is things like Google
Docs, Google Drive.

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:50.150
That is the software as a
service side of the house.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:52.970
And so within the
umbrella of Google Cloud,

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:54.740
this is how we divide things up.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:56.570
On the right in
GCP land, we have

00:08:56.570 --> 00:09:00.230
infrastructure and platform, and
on the left, we have software.

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.540
Today we're only
talking about GCP,

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:07.070
but as you all probably know,
G Suite also has a ton of APIs

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:10.310
that you can use to
enhance the GCP experience.

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.990
And they use a lot of the
same consoles, for example.

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:16.360
And so you should really
look into that as well.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.450
So on GCP, you can
do a lot of things.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:21.689
First of all, you can run code.

00:09:21.689 --> 00:09:23.230
So you can spin up
a virtual machine,

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.410
and you can literally
just run a giant script

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:26.630
that'll take a week.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.380
Instead of running it
on your own computer

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.930
and worrying about the battery
dying and this and that,

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.270
you can just put it on a
virtual machine in the Cloud,

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:35.130
or you can deploy
a web application

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:37.200
and have the code run on Cloud.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.640
You can store data.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.170
So we have SQL databases.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.820
We have NoSQL databases.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.140
We have file storage.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.140
All of these things I use
almost every single day to build

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:46.890
web apps--

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:47.820
all these tools.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.380
And again, it's mostly all
fully managed and fully scaled.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:54.631
We have tools for
data migration.

00:09:54.631 --> 00:09:56.130
So this is for,
like, companies that

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.320
have a bunch of
data in, let's say,

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:00.570
disks in some closet
somewhere, and they

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:02.910
want to get that
information onto the Cloud.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:05.910
We have tools that
allow people to do that.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:07.352
We have tools for networking.

00:10:07.352 --> 00:10:09.060
So in less than a
minute, you can spin up

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.980
a world wide network--

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.350
literally from here to India.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.100
I could spin that up
in less than a second.

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:17.580
We have tools for networking--

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.130
I mean, sorry-- for monitoring.

00:10:19.130 --> 00:10:21.470
So let's say you have all
these tools in your Cloud,

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.370
in your platform, and you want
to monitor how they're working.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:26.870
If one thing crashes, let's
see why that happened.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:29.820
We have tools that
allow you to do that.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.800
We have tools that
help you build

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.570
Internet of Things projects.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:36.740
So you should look
into IoT Core.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:39.440
Also we have tools that
help administrators manage

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.960
this entire platform,
because projects

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:45.530
like we're going to see later on
with big companies like Spotify

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.260
and Coca-Cola, they have
a lot of stuff going on

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:51.510
in their Cloud, so they need
some way to manage all of that.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:54.270
We have tools that
help people do that.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:55.890
We have tools to
build your own API--

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:58.640
it's called Apiary--
and other things.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:00.190
And lastly, we have
tools that help

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:03.100
you build security
and administration

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:04.720
into your platform.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.320
So you can say, OK, I have
user data in my SQL table.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.240
My entire company
doesn't need to see

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:11.540
what's going on in there.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:14.950
So you can manage who can see
what within your platform using

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:17.180
the IAM and Admin tools.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:18.700
We also have some
open source tools

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:21.310
like TensorFlow, which is--
we have some sessions on that.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:23.730
It's a machine learning
platform, totally open source.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.670
Many other companies
use it as well.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:28.640
We have Kubernetes, as
well as other open source

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.890
tools that help with security
and management, as well.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.230
So now I've gone
over, what is Cloud?

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:38.274
And I've talked
about what GCP is.

00:11:38.274 --> 00:11:40.940
So now we're going to talk about
how some companies are actually

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:43.280
deploying these
tools and using them

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.430
to help them spend less
time managing and more time

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:47.990
creating more.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:52.415
So if we could just play the
video now, that'd be great.

00:11:52.415 --> 00:11:55.186
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:11:55.686 --> 00:11:57.990
- We wanted to make
this marketing campaign

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:00.420
for Coca-Cola something that
every single person in this

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.180
world, no matter who you are,
could participate in the World

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.590
Cup experience.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:05.480
Submit your photo.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.560
You could be on the pitch at the
opening ceremony of the World

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:09.060
Cup.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.700
- We're talking about 3
and 1/2 million images

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.800
from over 200 countries.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:17.340
Different formats, different
users, different sources.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:18.990
- Fans could actually
find their photo

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:21.620
to know, OK, I was here
on the flag in the opening

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.360
of the World Cup.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.710
- It was a gigantic scale.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:29.370
The agency that was creating the
photo mosaic printed in fabric.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:32.060
We recreated the
equivalent digitally.

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:35.250
We did not have time to
set up an infrastructure

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.220
and set up the servers and
configure the machines.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:40.500
By using Google
Cloud platform, we

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:43.410
went straight into
building on top of it.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.720
Without that, my
first deliverable

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:46.840
would be impossible.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:48.510
- You have to predict what
the loads are going to be.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:49.850
There's a lot of uncertainty.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.766
When you look at a cloud
provider like Google,

00:12:51.766 --> 00:12:53.700
the opportunity there
is for auto scaling.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.470
You don't have to worry
about disaster recovery.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.511
You don't have to worry
about backup because it's

00:12:57.511 --> 00:12:58.980
provided to you as a service.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.110
- We wanted to
send everyone that

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:05.640
had an image in that flag a
notification the very moment

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.600
the flag was opened
physically in the field.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:11.390
We would have a lot of people
doing that simultaneously.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:15.030
They're not taking
turns to use it.

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:19.450
So we were monitoring the
transmission on TV and waiting.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:23.170
I remember clearly seeing
the spikes and just hoping,

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:24.170
please, please scale up.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:25.730
Scale up.

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:26.847
And it did.

00:13:26.847 --> 00:13:29.430
- Fundamentally, it gets down
to one thing, and it's one word.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:30.360
It's trust.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.060
The same infrastructure that
powers the Google search engine

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.700
and search experience is
what's powering the cloud

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.040
that Coca-Cola is running on.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.490
- The biggest digital
marketing initiative.

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:46.975
- To participate is like a
once in a lifetime opportunity.

00:13:46.975 --> 00:13:50.412
