00:13:59.170 --> 00:13:59.670
Anyway.

00:13:59.670 --> 00:14:01.740
So who's heard of Coca-Cola?

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:02.490
Right.

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:05.370
So Coca-Cola, one of
the biggest companies

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:09.000
on the face of the earth,
put their biggest marketing

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:11.880
campaign in history in
the hands of Google.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:15.840
And as you saw, it went
very, very successfully.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.400
And so that's just one example.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.025
And now we're going
to see another one.

00:14:19.025 --> 00:14:22.070
So let's see how Spotify
used GCP to enhance the music

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:24.581
experience for everybody.

00:14:24.581 --> 00:14:25.080
Can we roll?

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:25.746
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:14:25.746 --> 00:14:27.170
- The mission at
Spotify is really

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:29.420
about being a great
musical companion

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:31.015
and making it more personal.

00:14:31.015 --> 00:14:33.140
It's not going to be the
streaming music experience

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:34.460
or the online music experience.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.650
It's going to be your
music experience.

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:40.040
- We create the tools for
people to actually swim

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:43.070
in that sea of
music data and find

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.650
the information that
really corresponds

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:47.510
to why they're there.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:51.050
- Quite while ago, we've made
a decision to focus on data.

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:53.060
- Depending on the
complexity of the query,

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.800
it could take a whole day.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.990
Sometimes it would
have to run overnight.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.330
- And being able to store
that and process that

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:00.950
has been a very big challenge.

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:02.840
Moving to Google's
Cloud platform

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:06.530
enabled us to take use of the
scale that Google can provide.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:09.770
- The reality is it's quite hard
to build great data centers,

00:15:09.770 --> 00:15:13.070
and we saw an opportunity to
spend our focus elsewhere.

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:16.310
And that was really the driver
into Google's Cloud platform.

00:15:16.310 --> 00:15:19.630
- With Google Cloud tools,
we can rerun the same query,

00:15:19.630 --> 00:15:22.280
and it might take a
few seconds, minutes.

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:24.080
- The less time
that we can spend

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:25.910
solving problems
that are already

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.190
solved, like scaling
our Hadoop cluster

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.840
when Google has
already figured out

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:31.940
how to scale it for us,
the more time and energy

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.820
we can spend on turning
our data into value.

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:36.507
And that's really a
game changer for us.

00:15:36.507 --> 00:15:38.090
When you're talking
about a technology

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:40.550
like BigQuery, when you're
talking about Dataflow,

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:43.670
you will feel the Spotify
product evolve, making the user

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:44.970
experience great.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:46.970
- Once you break
down the data, you

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:49.020
realize that there are nuances.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:50.510
It's not a straightforward path.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:54.620
It's a mosaic rich beyond
the most popular artists.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.480
It's a lot more than that.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:00.890
Music can transcend boundaries
of language, culture.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:03.740
If it moves you, then it's
there, and it's there forever.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:07.470
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:10.410
[END PLAYBACK]

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:16.200
ARMAN HEZARKHANI: So Spotify,
another gigantic company

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:21.150
that we all use, put their
success in Google's hands.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:23.880
They used Google's
tools to basically

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.130
get a bunch of their
data from users

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:29.520
and provide tools to their
users, like The Daily Mix,

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:32.280
or whatever else playlists
that are custom made for you.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:35.010
And if you heard in
the video, basically

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:38.220
the CTO said, why would
we try to solve a problem

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:39.810
that's already been solved?

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:43.570
Google was building the biggest
search engine in the world.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:45.420
And as a matter
of fact, they just

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:50.440
built one of the greatest sets
of data centers in the world.

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.140
So why would another
company put time

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:53.640
into doing that
when they could just

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:55.530
put more time into creating?

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:58.950
And so that's how
companies use GCP.

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:01.140
But how are students using GCP?

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:02.974
That's what we're
worried about today.

00:17:02.974 --> 00:17:04.890
So now we're going to
go through some projects

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.990
that students actually
built, and we're going

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:09.640
to see what tools they used.

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:11.400
So first, I'm going
to talk about a tool

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.980
that we found at the
University of Arizona

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:16.450
at a hackathon
called Hack Arizona.

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:20.579
So a few of us were at the
hackathon, and we were judging,

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:22.910
and we saw this
group of students.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:25.667
It was about three students,
and they were freshmen.

00:17:25.667 --> 00:17:27.750
And they'd just gotten
back from a trip to Europe,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.540
and they were telling us about
the horror stories of finding

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.490
tickets to and from Europe.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:35.400
If anybody's ever tried to find
tickets to Europe as a student,

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:37.920
you know how difficult
it is to find tickets

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:39.084
that are within our budget.

00:17:39.084 --> 00:17:40.500
And they basically
wanted to solve

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.251
this tool using technology.

00:17:42.251 --> 00:17:44.250
And so they showed up to
this hackathon and they

00:17:44.250 --> 00:17:46.170
said, how can we
build a tool that

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:48.750
will allow students
to find cheap flights

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:51.100
to and from Europe?

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:55.500
And if you know anything
about searching for flights,

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:58.626
you know that there is an
API out there or a few APIs

00:17:58.626 --> 00:18:00.000
out there where
you can literally

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.120
scour the internet for every
flight that's out there.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:06.580
It includes where it's going
from and where it's going to.

00:18:06.580 --> 00:18:08.070
It includes the
price, the times,

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:09.840
all the information you need.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:12.750
And so they basically
queried this API,

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:16.470
and now they had a gigantic
data set and a very difficult

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:17.310
problem.

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:20.632
Because parsing this--
basically, it's like a tree.

00:18:20.632 --> 00:18:22.590
It's like a tree with
nodes of different places

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:23.490
where you're going.

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:25.890
And parsing this and
trying to find the cheapest

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:28.900
flight, that's what's called
the traveling salesman problem,

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.910
and that is a very, very
computationally heavy problem,

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:34.440
as some of you may
know if you study CS.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.540
And so they thought, how
are we going to do this?

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.250
Because no one's ever
solved this problem

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.100
in less than exponential
time, and so it's very, very

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.660
computationally heavy.

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:46.080
Let's just throw it
on Google Cloud's VMs.

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:49.024
They spun up, like, the
biggest and most expensive

00:18:49.024 --> 00:18:50.190
virtual machine that we had.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.110
It had, like, 16
cores or something.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:53.170
It was insane.

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.330
But they spun it up.

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:57.060
And when you go onto
this web application,

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:00.600
you don't even notice that
it's doing this gigantic search

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:03.720
because of how strong
and powerful Google's

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:04.800
infrastructure is.

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:07.830
And the code that they wrote
was also pretty efficient.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:11.590
And so that's one example of
a thing that we could all use

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.590
and we all could have
thought of as well,

00:19:13.590 --> 00:19:17.910
but it relies on GCP
to make it usable,

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.160
to make it something that
we would actually use.

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:22.590
And it saves us time.

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.540
Next, I want to talk
about a team that

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:26.405
was from Columbia University.

00:19:26.405 --> 00:19:28.530
They were basically solved
with this problem, which

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:31.530
is, how do we allow two
people to communicate

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:35.890
if one person is typing and
one person is on the phone?

00:19:35.890 --> 00:19:38.220
And so the problem
that they very quickly

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.750
faced was, what
language do these two

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:42.159
users speak to each other?

00:19:42.159 --> 00:19:44.200
And I'm not talking about,
like, spoken language.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.319
I'm talking about
one person's writing,

00:19:46.319 --> 00:19:47.360
the other one's speaking.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.410
Literally, how do we
put these two together?

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:52.960
And they also found
GCP at this hackathon,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.760
and this is what
they came up with.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:57.220
So this is a
diagram of the stack

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:58.636
that they built.
And I'm basically

00:19:58.636 --> 00:20:00.710
going to walk through
what's going on here.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:03.040
So on the top right,
you'll see Twilio.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:04.750
That's another
company that builds

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:08.290
an API that allows you to
use cell phones via code,

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:08.860
basically.

00:20:08.860 --> 00:20:10.894
And it spins you
up a phone number,

00:20:10.894 --> 00:20:12.310
and so you can
call, you can text,

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:14.580
and do a bunch of
things from the API.

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:16.150
On the left, we
have Hangouts Chat,

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:17.810
which is a Google
product, and then we

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:19.643
have Messenger, which
is a Facebook product.

00:20:19.643 --> 00:20:21.561
Those are both chat
messaging apps.

00:20:21.561 --> 00:20:22.810
So let's start with the right.

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:25.060
Let's start with,
how do I allow a user

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:29.110
to speak and turn that into
text and send that over

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:31.460
to a chat room?

00:20:31.460 --> 00:20:39.070
So we start with Twilio
sending a request basically

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:42.490
to an instance, which is
basically a virtual machine

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:46.870
or just, like, a computer
on Google Compute Engine.

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:49.840
So they had a server running
on Google Compute Engine that

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:51.370
was running a Flask server.

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.150
So Flask is a Python
framework, and it basically

00:20:55.150 --> 00:20:57.310
accepts this
request from Twilio.

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:58.900
And now they have audio message.

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:01.360
They have an audio file
that they then send

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:05.230
to Google's speech to text API.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.660
This API basically uses
Google's deep learning.

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:09.040
It's a fully trained model.

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:11.980
All you need to do is basically
call a method in your code

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:16.090
that says, almost literally,
turn this speech into text.

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:20.050
So it does that, and then
it sends the text back.

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:22.630
And then now in this server, we
have text, which is basically

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:24.910
just a string, and
then we send that

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:27.760
to another server that's
also running a Flask app,

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:30.430
but that handles the
text side of this stack.

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:32.410
And then once we have
the text over there,

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.240
we send that up
to the chat rooms,

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.430
whoever this person
is speaking to.

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:41.170
So now using this API and
using the Google Compute Engine

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:44.099
instance, we were able to
turn the voice into text.

00:21:44.099 --> 00:21:45.640
So now let's go the
other way around.

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:52.230
So we get text into the
server, and then we send that

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:54.607
into the Text to Speech API.

00:21:54.607 --> 00:21:56.190
And if any of you
saw the presentation

00:21:56.190 --> 00:22:01.290
that Sundar, our CEO, gave
at, I think it was Google I/O,

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.630
then you probably
saw the fully--

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.034
like, the speaking robot.

00:22:05.034 --> 00:22:06.450
They were using
this deep learning

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.760
model, which
basically turns text

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:11.700
into very recognizable speech.

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:14.010
It sounds like you're
talking to a person.

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:18.270
And so we call that API, which
sends us back this audio.

00:22:18.270 --> 00:22:20.640
We send that audio
to the other server,

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:24.540
which sends it to Twilio and
will respond on the phone.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:28.800
And so now we have a full
communication platform

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:31.680
between text and speech.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:33.180
So I have a little
check mark there.

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:34.263
We just accomplished that.

00:22:34.263 --> 00:22:35.050
Check.

00:22:35.050 --> 00:22:38.350
So this team built this in
a hackathon using our tools.

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:40.600
And if any of you know about
hackathons, which you do,

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:43.020
they're usually under 36 hours.

00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:44.940
So it means that
these tools are pretty

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:46.440
easy to use if
somebody could just

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.779
get it done in under 36 hours.

00:22:48.779 --> 00:22:50.320
And so we're very
proud of this team.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:51.840
They built something
really cool.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:55.046
And these were two examples of
something that students built.

00:22:55.046 --> 00:22:56.545
And it's basically
up to y'all, too,

00:22:56.545 --> 00:22:59.280
to get these tools into the
hands of students throughout

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:00.035
the next year.

00:23:00.035 --> 00:23:02.160
And we're really excited
to see what other projects

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:05.710
we can find from your work.

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:07.295
And lastly, how
do I get started?

00:23:07.295 --> 00:23:09.420
So now we're actually going
to get our hands dirty.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:11.550
Now it's time to take
out your laptops.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:13.824
And as we're going
through this demo,

00:23:13.824 --> 00:23:15.240
there are going
to be some helpers

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.360
walking around and basically
answering questions

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:19.890
if you have them.

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:22.590
And in addition to that,
you each have a coupon code

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:29.010
in front of you, which
looks something like this.

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:30.630
And on the side of
this coupon code,

00:23:30.630 --> 00:23:33.000
you'll see a little
yellow bar with a number.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.940
And I'll talk about
that a little bit later.

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:39.280
So right now we're
going to do four things.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:41.130
First, we're going to log in.

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:42.840
Then, we're going
to make a project,

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:45.250
and I'll explain
what that means.

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:47.160
We're going to create
a billing account,

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:48.960
and then we're going
to create a VM.

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:50.820
So now I'm going to
log onto my computer,

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:52.445
and we're going to
do all these things.

00:23:56.316 --> 00:23:58.440
So if we could just get
the computer on the screen.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:23:58.940
Please.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:01.460
OK.

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:02.270
Cool.

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:04.670
So this is my computer.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:07.970
So first and foremost, I
would like you all to log on

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:11.780
to console.cloud.google.com.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:13.560
So I'll type it in with you.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:15.200
console.cloud.google.com.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:19.610
And then just press Enter.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:24.110
And you'll come to a page that
says Access Forbidden that

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:27.150
will look a little bit--

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:28.187
I'll just come here.

00:24:28.187 --> 00:24:29.770
That will look a
little bit like this,

00:24:29.770 --> 00:24:31.395
but it'll have some
other stuff saying,

00:24:31.395 --> 00:24:32.982
sign up for a $300 credit.

00:24:32.982 --> 00:24:34.690
It might say that if
it's your first time

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:36.520
logging onto the console.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:39.862
But I've already logged on to
this, so it looks like this.

00:24:39.862 --> 00:24:41.320
The first thing
we're going to do--

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:45.220
also, if you haven't logged
in, just use a Gmail account.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:46.720
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:24:46.720 --> 00:24:49.970
is we're going to
say Select a Project.

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:53.240
And then now we have all
these different projects.

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.180
And so what is a project?

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:57.640
Let's say I'm a
company or a student,

00:24:57.640 --> 00:24:59.650
and I'm working at
a hackathon, and I

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.290
want to build that flight tool.

00:25:02.290 --> 00:25:05.634
So within that flight tool,
they had a web app running,

00:25:05.634 --> 00:25:07.300
they had some storage
instances running,

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.580
and they had a few different
Google Cloud Platform

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:10.570
resources.

00:25:10.570 --> 00:25:11.960
So the way that
we house that all

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:15.640
together with the permissions
and everything is in a project.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.130
So it's quite intuitive.

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.540
A project is a project.

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:24.070
So to make a project,
we click on New Project.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:27.311
And what it's asking us
for is a project name.

00:25:27.311 --> 00:25:28.810
You can name this
anything you want.

00:25:28.810 --> 00:25:31.060
And if you're, like,
incorrect or are not

00:25:31.060 --> 00:25:32.682
in the correct
format, it'll tell you

00:25:32.682 --> 00:25:33.890
and then you could change it.

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:36.520
But I'm just going to
stay with the default.

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:38.800
And then it's going to
say No Organization.

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:40.150
That's fine for now.

00:25:40.150 --> 00:25:41.804
And then Create.

00:25:41.804 --> 00:25:43.720
So what it's doing right
now is it's basically

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:44.920
making my project.

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:46.900
It takes a minute because
it needs to propagate

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:48.610
through all the systems.

00:25:48.610 --> 00:25:51.221
But as we wait for
that to happen, again,

00:25:51.221 --> 00:25:52.720
if you have any
questions, there are

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:55.090
people standing around who
can answer your questions, so

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:57.530
feel free to raise your
hand if you need it.

00:25:57.530 --> 00:26:00.340
So now we have our project.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:02.350
As you can see, this
is our dashboard,

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:04.570
and right now it
is fairly empty.

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:07.060
There is no sign of any
errors for whatever.

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:09.640
It's all empty.

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:11.830
So as you use it, this
stuff will get filled up

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:14.080
with some monitoring
and some tools that

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:17.260
will help you see all of
your resources at a glance.

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:19.450
But the one thing I'm going
to highlight right now

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:22.280
is at the top left
of the screen.

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:25.090
So up here, we see Project Info.

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:28.400
And I can zoom into this
a couple clicks as well.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.950
So we have three
pieces of information.

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:32.977
The two most important
are at the top,

00:26:32.977 --> 00:26:35.060
and the one most important
is right in the middle,

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:37.290
and that is your Project ID.

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:40.610
So your project name is
not universally unique,

00:26:40.610 --> 00:26:42.840
but the Project ID is.

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:46.280
And so the project ID oftentimes
when you're using GCP tools,

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:47.339
you'll be asked for it.

00:26:47.339 --> 00:26:49.130
And the way that you'll
find that is you'll

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:51.770
just click on Google Cloud
Platform, which will bring you

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:53.240
back to the Dashboard.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:55.850
You'll make sure that you're
in the correct project,

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:57.960
and then you'll just
find it right here.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:00.620
So if you ever need
your project ID,

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:02.880
you can just go right there.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:05.990
So next, let's hook up billing.

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:08.360
So GCP costs money.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:11.750
So companies pay us money to
basically use these tools.

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:15.270
But as students, as
was mentioned before,

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:17.180
we have these free
credits that we

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:20.240
can use to use these tools
for free to get started.

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:25.010
So these coupons each have
a code on the bottom in--

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:27.170
I guess that's
like an orange hue.

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.090
And so the way that
you enter these is you

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:35.070
go to console.cloud.google.com,
same as before,

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:37.700
but you do slash edu.

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:43.802
So that's
console.cloud.google.com/edu.

00:27:43.802 --> 00:27:45.260
And so you go to
this page, and you

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:48.770
see that there's a little form
specific to education grants.

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:50.610
And these are the ones
that we have here.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:52.420
And so what we do is
we enter this code.

00:27:52.420 --> 00:27:54.050
So I'm going to do
this live right now.

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:01.615
So we can all enter
these together.

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.820
And then once I'm done entering
it, instead of the red,

00:28:07.820 --> 00:28:11.407
we see this credit amount
expiration date and course.

00:28:11.407 --> 00:28:12.490
So Michael, where are you?

00:28:12.490 --> 00:28:13.519
Wave to us.

00:28:13.519 --> 00:28:14.060
Hey, Michael.

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:16.340
Michael's name is on the board.

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:19.730
So now we have this coupon code.

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:20.820
One quick thing.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:23.402
Oftentimes, if you're at an
event and you have these codes

00:28:23.402 --> 00:28:24.860
and you're handing
them out, people

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:27.485
will say, oh, like, this code is
still red, even though they're

00:28:27.485 --> 00:28:28.092
done typing.

00:28:28.092 --> 00:28:30.050
The first thing that they
probably did was they

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:31.340
just had a typo.

00:28:31.340 --> 00:28:34.400
But otherwise, it could be that
they already used the coupon

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:35.540
or they already entered it.

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:37.947
And so if you ever need
to debug that problem,

00:28:37.947 --> 00:28:39.530
it's probably one
of those two things.

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:44.170
They either mistyped it or
they already used that code.

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:47.210
And so for country of residence,
I'm just going to say US.

00:28:47.210 --> 00:28:49.170
And then please
email me updates.

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:51.660
So these are not, like, very
annoying marketing updates.

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:53.660
They're actually things
specific to education.

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:57.620
So I love education and I'm
going to click Yes, as you

00:28:57.620 --> 00:28:59.690
all of education.

00:28:59.690 --> 00:29:01.947
And then I click
Accept and Continue.

00:29:01.947 --> 00:29:03.530
So what this does
is it creates what's

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:06.480
called a billing account.

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:10.870
And so the idea of a billing
account versus a project are--

00:29:10.870 --> 00:29:12.560
they're kind of two
different things.

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:16.590
So every project needs a
billing account hooked up to it,

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:18.620
but I can have one
billing account that's

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:20.790
hooked up to several projects.

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:23.060
So let's say I enter
my credit card.

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.770
And I have one project for
a side project I'm doing,

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:28.010
and then another one
for a class project.

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:29.840
I can have that one
billing account tied

00:29:29.840 --> 00:29:31.950
to these multiple projects.

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:35.120
And so the way that you hook
them up to the actual project

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:39.215
is you click on this dropdown
menu right at the top,

00:29:39.215 --> 00:29:41.215
and then you go down to
Manage Billing Accounts.

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:48.400
So now we're on the
Manage Billing Accounts.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.690
And then up here we see--

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:50.620
I'll zoom in a little.

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:53.680
We see My Billing
Accounts and My Projects.

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:55.390
Right now we want to
go to the Projects

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:59.050
so that we can specify what
billing account we want to use.

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:00.880
So we go to My Projects.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:04.430
And over here we see this is
the project that I just spun up.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:07.350
Daring Diode.

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:09.419
Yeah.

00:30:09.419 --> 00:30:10.710
MICHAEL HAMAMOTO: Really quick.

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:12.900
If anyone's having
trouble when they're

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.710
trying to create a new project
and it's saying organization

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:19.170
and there's the red
text, what that means,

00:30:19.170 --> 00:30:22.710
without getting into specifics,
try using your personal Gmail

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:23.610
account.

00:30:23.610 --> 00:30:26.850
Some people's school domains,
like your .edu account,

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:29.560
aren't activated to use GCP.

00:30:29.560 --> 00:30:34.120
And so use your personal
Gmail if that's the case.

00:30:34.120 --> 00:30:37.260
And then once you've done that,
if you're still having issues,

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:39.970
raise your hand, and we'll
come and try and help you.

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:40.400
ARMAN HEZARKHANI: Cool.

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:42.030
Yeah, and that's also a
very good point, Michael.

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.196
That's something that a lot
of people at your school

00:30:44.196 --> 00:30:44.880
will also face.

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:47.110
We run into this with
faculty all the time.

00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:49.380
And so if that is
an issue, that means

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:51.990
that your G Suite
administrator, so the IT people

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:54.660
at your school,
didn't allow GCP to be

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:57.217
used in this organization.

00:30:57.217 --> 00:30:58.800
So if you just use
your Gmail account,

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:00.210
everything will be fine.

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:01.819
So we're here,
and right now this

00:31:01.819 --> 00:31:03.610
is already hooked up
to the billing account

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:04.830
because I already had one.

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:06.630
But for you to add
a billing account,

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:08.850
you click on this
dot dot dot thing,

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.339
and you click Change
Billing Account,

00:31:11.339 --> 00:31:12.630
and then you can just pick one.

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:13.920
So this says No Billing
Accounts Available

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:15.310
because I already have one.

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:19.260
But you click on that dot dot
dot, you click Change Billing

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:21.476
Account, and then you would
click the actual billing

00:31:21.476 --> 00:31:23.100
account that you want
to hook it up to,

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:25.860
which would be called Michael
Hamamoto or something.

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:29.520
And so now we go
back to our project,

00:31:29.520 --> 00:31:31.250
and we have a billing
account in here,

00:31:31.250 --> 00:31:32.760
and we have our project set up.

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:34.385
So let's actually do
something in here.

00:31:34.385 --> 00:31:36.090
Let's actually build a resource.

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:38.530
So we're going to do what's
called spinning up a VM.

00:31:38.530 --> 00:31:40.530
So we're just going to
create a virtual machine.

00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:43.113
And for those of you who don't
know what a virtual machine is,

00:31:43.113 --> 00:31:46.260
it's basically, like,
an abstraction away

00:31:46.260 --> 00:31:47.780
from an actual computer.

00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:49.530
So you all have computers
in front of you,

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:51.180
and they all have
CPUs in them that's

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:52.660
actually running the code.

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:55.320
So we have a bunch
of CPUs at GCP,

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:57.360
but we don't sell
those, exactly.

00:31:57.360 --> 00:31:59.400
We sell what's called a
virtual machine, which

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:03.330
is running on this actual CPU.

00:32:03.330 --> 00:32:06.930
So we can have like three or
four VMs running on one CPU,

00:32:06.930 --> 00:32:10.000
and they're kind of
separated operating systems.

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:11.880
So we're going to do that.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:14.550
First thing we're going to do is
click on the top left hamburger

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:15.450
menu.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:18.460
So this is like if you
ever need anything,

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:20.790
you just click on this
and it's probably there.

00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:24.870
This is, like, the answer
to all of my problems ever.

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:27.790
That's not exactly
true, but I hope it was.

00:32:27.790 --> 00:32:28.774
So you're over here.

00:32:28.774 --> 00:32:30.190
As you can see, a
lot of the tools

00:32:30.190 --> 00:32:34.920
I spoke about earlier like
IAM, and Admin, and App Engine,

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:37.690
and storage, and all these
things, they're all here.

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:39.705
So if you ever want to
use one of these tools,

00:32:39.705 --> 00:32:42.330
you click on the hamburger menu,
and then you scroll down to it

00:32:42.330 --> 00:32:43.442
and find it there.

00:32:43.442 --> 00:32:45.150
What we're going to
do now, though, we're

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:49.110
going to use Compute
Engine, which shorthand,

00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:51.390
you'd just say GCE.

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:53.920
So you go to Compute Engine,
you just click on that,

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:56.710
and then it says Compute
Engine is Getting Ready.

00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:58.500
So as you can see,
there's a lot of times

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:01.050
when GCP is just kind
of propagating data

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:02.370
through its systems.

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:04.036
And so that's what
it's doing right now.

00:33:04.036 --> 00:33:05.460
It's just getting ready for us.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.386
But once that happens, then we
can just get ready and do that.

00:33:08.386 --> 00:33:10.260
And again, I see, like,
people are continuing

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:11.135
to raise their hands.

00:33:11.135 --> 00:33:12.360
Keep doing that.

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:15.020
Our helpers are running around
and answering questions.

00:33:15.020 --> 00:33:17.880
Just keep your hand up
if you have a question,

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:20.050
and we'll get to you.

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:23.340
